    <bpmn:process id="W_W1025" name="W1025. Store-Level Shelving, Racking &amp; Display Fixture Safety Inspection &amp; Maintenance" isExecutable="false">
      <bpmn:documentation>Trigger: Quarterly scheduled inspection; also triggered by visible damage, after seismic event, or after forklift/vehicle impact
Frequency: Quarterly inspection × 200 stores = 800 inspections/year; ad-hoc for incidents
Volume: ~3,000–5,000 shelving units per store (including gondola end-caps, wall shelving, pallet racking in backroom, outdoor yard racks)
Owner: Assistant Store Manager
Participants: Maintenance/Utility Staff, Department Supervisors, Safety Committee Representative, Vendor Service Technician (for specialty fixtures)
Source: workflow W1025 — Store-Level Shelving, Racking &amp; Display Fixture Safety Inspection &amp; Maintenance</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:laneSet id="W1025_LaneSet5">
        <bpmn:lane id="W1025_Lane1" name="Maintenance Staff">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1025_Task7</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1025_Task9</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1025_Start6</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="W1025_Lane2" name="Department Supervisors">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1025_Task8</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="W1025_Lane3" name="Store Manager">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1025_Task10</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="W1025_Lane4" name="Vendor Technician">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1025_Task11</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1025_End12</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:startEvent id="W1025_Start6" name="Quarterly scheduled inspection; also triggered by visible damage, after seismic event, or…">
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1025_Flow15</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1025_Task7" name="Quarterly Visual Inspection">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 1: Quarterly Visual Inspection: Maintenance/Utility Staff and Department Supervisors conduct systematic walkthrough: (a) Gondola shelving (retail floor): check upright posts for leaning or buckling, shelf clips/bolts for tightness, base plates secure on floor, shelf boards for sagging or cracking, end-cap stability; (b) Pallet racking (backroom): check upright columns for damage (dent, bend, twist — any deformity &gt; 3mm requires replacement), beam connectors locked, safety pins in place, floor anchors secure, rack labeling intact; (c) Cantilever racks (lumber yard): check arms for bending under load, base bolted to concrete pad, no corrosion at weld points, load within rated capacity per arm; (d) Specialty fixtures: tile display racks (heavy — verify base anchoring), tool pegboard walls (verify mount integrity), appliance display platforms (verify level and stable); (e) record all findings in shelving inspection module — each unit scanned by location barcode with pass/fail/needs-repair status
Responsible (R): Maintenance Staff / Department Supervisors
Accountable (A): Assistant Store Manager
Duration: 4–6 hours (full store)</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1025_Flow15</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1025_Flow16</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1025_Task8" name="Load Capacity Verification">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 2: Load Capacity Verification: For any shelving unit flagged as overloaded: (a) check actual shelf load against rated capacity (rated capacity sticker must be visible on each unit — applied during installation per W225); (b) common overload issues in BuildRight: cement bags on gondola shelves (50 kg bags × 10 bags = 500 kg — exceeds most gondola ratings of 150–200 kg per shelf), tile cartons stacked above maximum height, heavy power tools on upper shelves; (c) re-distribute overloaded shelves immediately: move heavy items to lower shelves or pallet racking; (d) update planogram system (W314) with weight-class restrictions per shelf level — heavy items on bottom shelves only
Responsible (R): Department Supervisors / Stock Associates
Accountable (A): Assistant Store Manager
Duration: 1–2 hours</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1025_Flow16</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1025_Flow17</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1025_Task9" name="Repair &amp; Component Replacement">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 3: Repair &amp; Component Replacement: For units flagged as &quot;needs-repair&quot;: (a) Minor repairs (shelf clip replacement, bolt tightening, shelf board replacement): Maintenance Staff completes same day using on-hand spare parts inventory (each store maintains a spare parts kit: shelf clips, bolts, shelf boards, safety pins); (b) Moderate repairs (upright post replacement, beam replacement): Maintenance Staff orders replacement parts from facilities vendor per W47 (Store Facility Maintenance); racking temporarily unloaded and tagged &quot;out of service&quot; until repaired (within 5 business days); (c) Major structural issues (multiple bent uprights, anchor failure, weld cracking): Assistant Store Manager contacts Regional Facilities Manager and Engineering team per W789 for structural assessment; affected area cordoned off until engineer clears; (d) all repairs logged in system with parts used, labor hours, and repair date; (e) repair costs tracked per store for capital planning per W700
Responsible (R): Maintenance Staff / Vendor Technician
Accountable (A): Assistant Store Manager
Duration: 1–8 hours depending on severity</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1025_Flow17</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1025_Flow18</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1025_Task10" name="Post-Earthquake Emergency Inspection">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 4: Post-Earthquake Emergency Inspection: After any seismic event of Magnitude 5.0+ in store&#x27;s region (PHIVOLCS alert): (a) Store Manager initiates immediate shelving safety check before reopening after evacuation per W330; (b) focused on: upright post alignment (laser level check for plumb), beam connection integrity, anchor bolt condition, any displaced inventory indicating racking movement; (c) any compromised racking unloaded and cordoned off immediately; (d) Engineering team provides remote assessment via photos within 4 hours; (e) if damage is extensive, store may open with restricted access to affected areas
Responsible (R): Store Manager / Maintenance Staff
Accountable (A): Store Manager
Duration: 1–3 hours</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1025_Flow18</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1025_Flow19</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1025_Task11" name="Annual Deep Inspection &amp; Corrosion Assessment">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 5: Annual Deep Inspection &amp; Corrosion Assessment: Once per year (recommended: start of rainy season, June): (a) comprehensive structural inspection of all racking systems by qualified vendor technician; (b) corrosion assessment: measure steel thickness at critical weld points and base plates using ultrasonic thickness gauge; flag any thickness reduction &gt; 15% from baseline; (c) for coastal stores (within 2 km of coastline): accelerated corrosion expected — recommend galvanized or stainless steel replacement components; (d) load testing: verify cantilever rack and pallet racking load capacity with calibrated test weights; (e) seismic readiness: verify all racking is seismically braced per Philippine structural code (NSCP 2015); (f) generate annual shelving condition report per store for capital planning (W21) and insurance (W857)
Responsible (R): Vendor Technician / Maintenance Staff
Accountable (A): Regional Facilities Manager
Duration: 6–8 hours per store</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1025_Flow19</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1025_Flow20</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="W1025_End12" name="Complete">
        <bpmn:incoming>W1025_Flow20</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="W1025_Annotation13">
        <bpmn:text>Controls: - CTL-260 (ensure controlled execution — Store Facility &amp; Safety (PA-07.2).) | - operational: standard operating procedures; system-enforced validation rules
Owner: Assistant Store Manager</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
